.TH SYSMON 1 "November 2026" "sysmon" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
sysmon \- graphical system monitor for Raspberry Pi
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sysmon
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B sysmon
shows live rolling charts of CPU utilization, disk I/O, network I/O,
CPU and drive temperatures, and fan speed, updating every second with
60 seconds of history. It requires a graphical desktop session.
.PP
Drive temperatures are read with
.BR "sudo smartctl" ,
so the user must be able to run
.B smartctl
through
.B sudo
without a password.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/sysmon/sysmon.ini
Per-user settings: drives and network interfaces to hide, an optional
case fan tachometer GPIO pin, and
.B smartctl
device types. Edit them with
.B File \(-> Settings
in sysmon, or by hand. The file is optional: anything it does not set
follows built-in defaults worked out from the hardware at every start
(the loopback interface
.B lo
is hidden; if the root filesystem is on an NVMe drive,
.B mmcblk0
is hidden; on an Argon ONE UP,
.B eth0
is hidden). Because
.B smartctl
runs as root, a
.B [smartctl]
value may only be
.BI "-d " TYPE ;
anything else is ignored. If
.B $XDG_CONFIG_HOME
is set, the file is in
.IR $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/sysmon/ .
An annotated example is installed at
.IR /usr/share/doc/sysmon/examples/sysmon.ini .
.TP
.I /etc/sysmon.ini
Used by older versions. If it exists and the per-user file does not,
it is copied to the per-user file the first time sysmon runs, and is
not read after that.
.TP
.I /usr/lib/sysmon/venv
Python virtual environment the application runs in, created when the
package is configured.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR smartctl (8)
